 number, boolean or `null` — replaces `target` entirely, since RFC 7386 only merges object members and
 treats every other JSON value as a wholesale replacement (so, unlike `applyPatch`, array members are
 always replaced as a whole, never merged element by element).
+
+### `createPatch(from, to)`
+
+Computes a [JSON Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6902) (RFC 6902) that turns `from` into `to`
+when passed to `applyPatch`. Neither `from` nor `to` is mutated.
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch, applyPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+createPatch({ foo: 'bar' }, { foo: 'bar', baz: 'qux' }); // [{ op: 'add', path: '/baz', value: 'qux' }]
+createPatch({ foo: [1, 2, 3] }, { foo: [1, 5, 3] }); // [{ op: 'replace', path: '/foo/1', value: 5 }]
+```
+
+The patch describes only what changed: unchanged object members and array elements are left out of it
+entirely, nested objects and array elements that changed are diffed recursively rather than replaced
+wholesale, and arrays are compared by their longest common subsequence so that an element inserted or
+removed in the middle does not turn into replacements of every element after it. Applying the result
+of `createPatch(from, to)` to `from` with `applyPatch` always produces `to`.
