@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+---
+id: dl-005-createpatch-array-diffing-lcs-plus-positional-recursive-pairing
+type: decision-log
+title: "createPatch array diffing: LCS plus positional recursive pairing"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Context
+
+`createPatch(from, to)` (RFC 6902) must produce a patch that "describes what changed, and nothing
+more" — the task explicitly rules out the trivial implementation of always emitting a single
+`replace` at the root with the whole of `to`. RFC 6902 itself defines no diffing algorithm (it only
+defines what a patch, once produced, does when applied), so every choice below is this
+implementation's own.
+
+Two sub-problems needed a design, not just a correctness fix:
+
+1. **Array diffing.** Comparing `from` and `to` element-by-element at the same index is wrong as
+   soon as an element is inserted or removed anywhere but the end: every element after that point
+   gets compared against the wrong counterpart and looks "changed," producing a `replace` for each
+   of them instead of one `add`/`remove` at the insertion point.
+2. **What to do with an array element, or an object member present in both sides, that differs.**
+   Replacing it wholesale with `to`'s value is correct but can be far from minimal when the old and
+   new values are themselves large, similar structures (e.g. an object in an array where only one
+   field changed) — the exact "resending the whole thing for a small change" problem the task calls
+   out, just one level down instead of at the document root.
+
+## Decision
+
+1. **Objects diff member-by-member, recursing into members present on both sides** rather than
+   comparing member values directly to decide add/remove/replace at that one level. A member missing
+   from `to` is a `remove`; missing from `from` is an `add` of `to`'s value; present on both sides is
+   diffed recursively at its child path (`createPatch` calls itself on the member values), so the
+   patch only ever contains a `replace` once recursion bottoms out at values that are not both
+   objects or both arrays.
+
+2. **Arrays diff by longest common subsequence (LCS)**, using the same recursive deep-equality check
+   already used elsewhere in this module, computed with the standard O(n·m) dynamic-programming
+   table, then walked once to classify each element as "kept" (part of the LCS — emits nothing),
+   "removed," or "inserted." This correctly isolates a middle insertion/removal to a single
+   `add`/`remove` regardless of how long the array is, because elements on either side of the change
+   that are equal are recognized as the same element having moved, not as having changed.
+
+3. **Runs of removed/inserted elements between two LCS matches are paired up positionally and diffed
+   recursively**, not emitted as a flat `remove` of every old element followed by an `add` of every
+   new one. The k-th element removed in a run is diffed against the k-th element inserted in that
+   same run (in each side's left-to-right order, independent of the order the LCS walk happened to
+   decide them in) by calling the same `createPatch`-level recursion used for object members, at the
+   array index the result occupies; this is what turns `createPatch({ items: [{ id: 1, name: 'a' }] },
+   { items: [{ id: 1, name: 'b' }] })` into a single `replace` of `/items/0/name` instead of a
+   `replace` of the whole element at `/items/0`. Only a run with unequal counts of removed vs.
+   inserted elements falls back to plain `remove`/`add` for the surplus, since there is nothing
+   sensible to pair the extra elements against.
+
+4. **`replace`, not `add`, for a whole-value rewrite** — used both for the degenerate case at the
+   bottom of the object/array recursion (values that are not both objects or both arrays, or that
+   differ in kind) and for the document root itself (path `""`) when `from` and `to` are unrelated in
+   shape. Every one of these locations is one that `applyPatch` already treats as "existing" (an
+   object/array member known to be present on both sides, or the root, which `applyPatch` always
+   finds via its `{ doc: ... }` holder regardless of what `from` was), so `replace`'s RFC 6902
+   requirement that the target already exist is always satisfied; there is no case here where `add`
+   would be needed instead.
+
+Member names are escaped with the existing RFC 6901 escaping rule (`~` → `~0`, `/` → `~1`) before
+being appended to a path, the inverse of the unescaping `resolvePointer`/`applyPatch` already apply
+when consuming a pointer.
+
+No deep clone of `from` or `to` was needed for immutability: the diff only reads from both documents
+(`Object.keys`, plain indexing on an own, already-enumerated key — never a write), and the operations
+it emits reference values read out of `to`, never splice anything into `from`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The patch `createPatch` produces is close to minimal for the common cases this project's clients
+  are expected to hit (a single changed field, an appended/removed/inserted array element, a changed
+  element inside an array of records keyed by position), without needing a general-purpose
+  diff/patience algorithm or any notion of element identity (no "match by id field" heuristic): two
+  array elements are only ever considered "the same" when they are deeply equal.
+- A consequence of LCS-by-deep-equality with no identity heuristic: two structurally similar but not
+  deeply-equal elements that happen to land in the same positional "run" get the recursive-pairing
+  treatment above and so still diff minimally, but if a change also shifts which elements are deeply
+  equal to which (e.g. two elements swap values), the LCS may legitimately choose a different, still
+  globally-shortest, add/remove assignment than a human would — this is accepted as inherent to
+  position-based array diffing without an identity key, not a bug to fix later.
+- `createPatch` and `applyPatch` are complementary by construction (round-trip:
+  `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` equals `to`), but nothing enforces that at the type level;
+  a future change to one's semantics (e.g. a new operation kind) should re-check the other.
