@@ -0,0 +1,60 @@
+---
+id: task-005-implement-json-patch-rfc-6902-createpatch-diff
+type: task
+title: "Implement JSON Patch RFC 6902 createPatch (diff)"
+status: approved
+---
+
+## Request
+
+Clients now want to send only what changed between two versions of a JSON document, instead of the
+whole new version. Add a way to produce such a change as a JSON Patch (RFC 6902): export
+`createPatch(from, to)` from `src/index.ts`, returning a patch that turns `from` into `to` when applied
+via `applyPatch`, without mutating either input. The patch must describe what changed and nothing
+more — a small difference must not turn into resending the whole document.
+
+## What was done
+
+Implemented `createPatch(from, to)` in `src/index.ts` as a structural diff, recursing wherever both
+sides share the same shape so the patch stays local to the actual difference:
+
+- **Objects**: diffed member by member. A key missing from `to` becomes a `remove`; a key missing
+  from `from` becomes an `add` with `to`'s value; a key present in both is diffed recursively at its
+  child path, rather than replaced wholesale, so a one-field change inside a nested object yields a
+  patch that only touches that field (`createPatch({ foo: { a: 1, b: 2 } }, { foo: { a: 1, b: 3 } })`
+  → `[{ op: 'replace', path: '/foo/b', value: 3 }]`, not a `replace` of the whole `foo` object).
+- **Arrays**: diffed by their longest common subsequence (LCS, by deep equality, via the standard
+  O(n·m) DP table), so elements shared between `from` and `to` are left out of the patch entirely —
+  including when the shared elements shift position because something was inserted or removed in the
+  middle (`createPatch({ foo: ['a', 'c'] }, { foo: ['a', 'b', 'c'] })` → a single `add` at `/foo/1`,
+  not a cascade of replacements for every element after the insertion point). Within a run of elements
+  that differ between an LCS match on either side, elements are paired up positionally and diffed
+  *recursively* rather than turned into a flat remove-then-add of the whole element — so a changed
+  element deep inside an array of objects still yields a patch local to the one field that changed
+  (`createPatch({ items: [{ id: 1, name: 'a' }] }, { items: [{ id: 1, name: 'b' }] })` →
+  `[{ op: 'replace', path: '/items/0/name', value: 'b' }]`). Only a surplus of removed or added
+  elements within such a run (unequal counts) falls back to plain `remove`/`add` operations.
+- **Everything else** (scalars, `null`, or a type change between object/array/scalar at the same
+  location, including the whole document at the root path `""`): a single `replace` with `to`'s value
+  at that path — there is no smaller way to describe "this became something unrelated."
+
+Member names are escaped per RFC 6901 (`~` → `~0`, `/` → `~1`) before being appended to a path, reusing
+the escaping rules `resolvePointer`/`applyPatch` already decode on the way in.
+
+Neither input is mutated: the diff only ever reads from `from` and `to` (via `Object.keys` and plain
+indexing, never writing through them), and the emitted patch holds references to values copied out of
+`to`, never a value spliced into `from`.
+
+See `dl-005` for the design decisions behind the array-diffing strategy (LCS plus positional
+recursive pairing) and why `replace` (not `add`) is used for whole-document and whole-member
+rewrites.
+
+Added `test/create-patch.test.ts`: empty-diff, add/remove/replace of a scalar member, recursive
+diffing into a changed nested object and a changed array-of-objects element, array append/remove/
+middle-insertion, path-escaping of member names containing `/` and `~`, a root-level `replace` when
+`from` and `to` are unrelated in shape, a non-mutation check on both inputs, and a round-trip property
+test (`applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` equals `to`) across a mix of object, array and
+root-level changes.
+
+Confirmed the full suite (68 tests) and `tsc --noEmit` are clean. Updated `README.md` with a
+`createPatch(from, to)` section matching the style of the existing API sections.
